{
  "result": "Every morning, the bakery on our corner opens before the sun. Its windows glow against the dark street, and the smell of bread reaches the bus stop before the first bus does. People gather there quietly, holding cups, checking watches, pretending they are not grateful for a little borrowed warmth.\n\nThe baker is a woman named Mara, who remembers everyone\u2019s order but never announces this talent. She simply places the right pastry in the right bag, as if coincidence had become a reliable employee. When children visit, she draws tiny stars on their napkins with a very blunt blue pencil.\n\nOne winter, a storm knocked out the power across the neighborhood. The buses stopped, phones died, and the usual hurry dissolved into uncertainty. Mara lit candles and opened the door anyway. She gave away yesterday\u2019s rolls and warmed soup on a camping stove. Strangers shared tables and traded family stories.\n\nBy afternoon, someone had brought a guitar. Someone else had found a deck of cards. A retired electrician explained the outage using sugar packets, while a little girl appointed herself official candle inspector. Nobody solved the storm, but the room felt less like a place to wait than a celebration.\n\nThe electricity returned after sunset, and the street resumed its familiar rhythm. Yet something small had shifted. People who once stood silently together began asking one another about work, gardens, and stubborn knees. Mara kept drawing stars. The bakery kept opening early. The neighborhood had learned its own name again.",
